[Music]  as we continue to mark one year since  the deadly january 6th attack we want to  look at the historical significance of  the storming of our capitol building  according to a recent cbs news poll  68 68 of americans more than two-thirds  believe that that attack was a sign of  increasing political violence to come  only a third saw it as an isolated  incident joining me now are cbs news  political contributor joel payne and cbs  news chief political analyst john  dickerson uh good morning to both of you  uh i'm very glad you're here to provide  some depth uh and some background to  what got us here joel i want to begin  with you we're at a moment politically  in this country where the two sides  don't just think the other side is wrong  they think the other side is a threat  immoral even how did we get to that  point how do we get out of it there's so  many root causes i think the interview  that you did with the two voters earlier  i think indicate some of those  institutional breakdowns right belief in  media belief in government those things  have all gone by the wayside over the  last two decades also when you just  consider the fact that thinking about um  you know january 6th so many  voters feel like every election is a  life or death event  they feel like if i lose if the other  party wins it means doom for me and i  think that is spell doom for our  democracy yeah john we are at a point  where it doesn't seem like there's a  referee in american life you know people  don't trust congress they don't trust  the courts they don't trust the media so  so who can step in but you also hear  people say hey we've had political  violence before how is this different  you know the to the referee point a lot  of american democracy relies on us to be  our own referees and what we've learned  on january 6th is is that when you  forget about all the the traditions and  rules that were sort of more agreements  when you let all those drop you allow  violence to rush in if you think about  political violence in america there was  a period where you showed you were a  partisan by fighting in the streets so  we have had that before and before the  civil war obviously but then even in the  in the 60s but that's not to say that  we've seen this before what was  extraordinary about january 6 is you've  never had a person of such high  political power you can't get any higher  than the president take such direct aim  at right at the heart of democracy and  what is the heart of democracy that you  can peacefully transfer power and that's  what president trump tried to undermine  both by inciting the rioters but also in  what he tried to do with the department  of justice in getting the the votes  overthrown and also what he tried to do  in the states and john what's  interesting is that if you look for the  first reference to a stolen or illegal  vote in the career of donald trump as a  politician it's the iowa caucus and then  as recently as last month press releases  again referring to a stolen election i  have a caucus in 2016 that's right and  then he said it in general election  consistently about the in 2016. so this  has been something  you know part of what january 6 was is a  lot of gasoline was thrown on the floor  and as you point out it was four years  before  even the event here and that gasoline  started to accumulate and part of that  also includes the idea that basically as  joel was talking about earlier that the  the when that the opposition was not the  opposition it was the enemy and that if  you dehumanize them once you dehumanize  them enough well then anything goes and  that's what we saw here so joel  when donald trump goes away or leaves  the stage of public life  is this all over does the threat recede  well fortunately not also when you think  about the individuals who are here last  january 6th these yes there were  everyday americans there were also  office holders people who are running  for office i think i saw a report that  nearly three dozen of those people maybe  even more are actually running for  office so you're talking about  individuals that took this ultimate act  who are going to be at the levers of  power and also the republican party has  gotten more extreme they have not backed  off there's no political incentive for  them to back off of their january 6th  position  do you want leaders to lead more or do  you have hope that these voters are  going to in their communities and  conversations amongst themselves come to  a different conclusion about the  country's future so this is a thing in  politics that i think we've always  struggled with is do the people lead the  leaders of the leaders lead the people  and i think in this situation you need  leaders who have guts to stand up it was  tough for mitt romney it was tough for  lindsey graham when they were harassed  at airports last year they've got to  have the gumption to deal with that to  push back on their voters and say this  is wrong they've got to have their own  john mccain moment like he had in 2008  on the campaign trail as karl rove  writes about in the wall street journal  today karl rove who's no shrinking  partisan he says the republicans have a  heavier burden when talking about  january 6. there were denunciations on  january 6th a year ago but a lot of  those have slipped into the background  and his argument is that republicans  need to be as clear today as they were  then about the fact that this was a  criminal terrorist attack that it was  incited by donald trump and that the  republican party can't survive if it has  truck with that [ __ ] yes the fbi is  investigating this as a domestic  terrorist incident to your point uh  gentlemen thank you both we appreciate  it

